Use `dynamicTool` for tools loaded at runtime, then check the `dynamic` flag on each tool call to distinguish them from static tools.

Example:

```ts
import { dynamicTool, generateText } from 'ai';
import { z } from 'zod';

// Load your tools from a config file at runtime
const runtimeTool = dynamicTool({
  description: 'A tool loaded from config',
  inputSchema: z.object({}), // or a minimal schema if you have one
  execute: async (input) => {
    // input is typed as 'unknown'; validate/cast at runtime
    const params = input as { action: string; args: unknown };
    return { result: `Ran ${params.action}` };
  },
});

// In your generateText call:
const result = await generateText({
  model: 'anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5',
  tools: { runtimeTool },
  onStepEnd: ({ toolCalls }) => {
    for (const toolCall of toolCalls) {
      if (toolCall.dynamic) {
        // Dynamic tool: input/output are 'unknown'
        console.log('Dynamic tool:', toolCall.toolName, toolCall.input);
      } else {
        // Static tool: full type inference
        console.log('Static tool:', toolCall.toolName, toolCall.input);
      }
    }
  },
});
```

Dynamic tools are identified at runtime via the `dynamic` flag on the `toolCall` object [1,2].

To make later steps receive updated tool context when a tool grants new permissions mid-run, use the `prepareStep` callback to return an updated `toolsContext` object. Tool context is immutable during execution, so you must update it between steps.

```ts
import { ToolLoopAgent, tool } from 'ai';
import { z } from 'zod';

const agent = new ToolLoopAgent({
  model: 'anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5',
  tools: {
    grantPermission: tool({
      description: 'Grant a new permission to the user',
      inputSchema: z.object({ permission: z.string() }),
      contextSchema: z.object({ grantedPermissions: z.array(z.string()) }),
      execute: async ({ permission }, { context }) => {
        // Grant the permission and return updated context
        return {
          grantedPermissions: [...context.grantedPermissions, permission],
        };
      },
    }),
  },
  toolsContext: {
    grantPermission: {
      grantedPermissions: [], // initial permissions
    },
  },
  prepareStep: async ({ steps, toolsContext }) => {
    const lastStep = steps.at(-1);

    // Check if the last step granted new permissions
    if (lastStep?.toolCalls?.some(t => t.toolName === 'grantPermission')) {
      // Return updated toolsContext with new permissions
      return {
        toolsContext: {
          grantPermission: {
            grantedPermissions: [
              ...toolsContext.grantPermission.grantedPermissions,
              // You would extract the actual granted permission from the tool result
            ],
          },
        },
      };
    }

    return {};
  },
});
```

[1][2][3][4]

No. By default, values passed through `toolsContext` (such as API keys) are **not** included in OpenTelemetry traces. You must explicitly configure `telemetry.includeToolsContext` to include specific properties, and even then only the properties you mark as `true` will appear in telemetry [1][2][3][4].

Example: if you omit the `telemetry.includeToolsContext` setting, the API key will not be sent to your telemetry provider:

```ts
const result = await generateText({
  model: "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5",
  tools: { weather: weatherTool },
  toolsContext: {
    weather: {
      weatherApiKey: 'secret-key', // not sent to telemetry
      defaultUnit: 'fahrenheit',
    },
  },
  prompt: 'What is the weather in San Francisco?',
  // telemetry.includeToolsContext omitted → nothing from toolsContext is sent
});
```

To keep spans and timing while excluding prompts and model outputs from traces, use the `@ai-sdk/otel` package and disable input/output recording in the telemetry settings:

```ts
import { OpenTelemetry } from '@ai-sdk/otel';
import { generateText } from 'ai';

const result = await generateText({
  model: 'openai/gpt-4o',
  prompt: 'Hello, world!',
  telemetry: {
    // Disable recording of inputs (prompts) and outputs (model responses)
    recordInputs: false,
    recordOutputs: false,
  },
  experimental_telemetry: {
    // Use the OpenTelemetry integration
    integration: new OpenTelemetry(),
  },
});
```

This configuration:
- Still emits spans and timing information through OpenTelemetry [1]
- Prevents prompts and model outputs from being included in the trace attributes (`gen_ai.input.messages`, `gen_ai.output.messages`, etc.) [1]
- Maintains the span structure for debugging multi-step tool calls [3]

When you pass `telemetry: { integrations: [DevToolsTelemetry()] }` on a per-call basis, it **replaces** the globally registered integrations for that specific call [2]. Since your per-call configuration only includes `DevToolsTelemetry()`, the globally registered `OpenTelemetry` integration is not active for that call, causing it to disappear from your OpenTelemetry traces.

To fix this, include both integrations in the per-call configuration:

```ts
import { generateText } from 'ai';
import { DevToolsTelemetry } from '@ai-sdk/devtools';
import { OpenTelemetry } from '@ai-sdk/otel';

const result = await generateText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Hello',
  telemetry: {
    integrations: [new OpenTelemetry(), DevToolsTelemetry()],
  },
});
```

Or remove the per-call configuration to use the globally registered integrations.

To see the raw provider **request** body in DevTools for `streamText`, include the `requestBody` option:

```ts
import { streamText } from 'ai';

const result = streamText({
  model: openai('gpt-4o'),
  prompt: 'Hello!',
  include: {
    requestBody: true, // only request body is available for streamText
  },
});
```

For `streamText`, **only the request body can be retained**—the response body is not captured in DevTools [1].

When you request `n: 10` from a model that only allows 1 image per call, the SDK automatically makes 10 separate API calls in parallel to generate all 10 images [1].

You can control the batching by setting `maxImagesPerCall` to override the default behavior. For example, to make 2 calls of 5 images each instead of 10 calls of 1 image each:

```ts
import { generateImage } from 'ai';

const { images } = await generateImage({
  model: "your-model",
  prompt: 'your prompt',
  n: 10,
  maxImagesPerCall: 5, // makes 2 calls of 5 images each
});
```

This parameter is documented in the API reference [2].

Generated images are returned in the `files` property of the `generateText` result object [1][2][8].

Example:
```ts
import { generateText } from 'ai';

const result = await generateText({
  model: 'google/gemini-3.1-flash-image-preview',
  prompt: 'Generate an image of a comic cat',
});

for (const file of result.files) {
  if (file.mediaType.startsWith('image/')) {
    // Access the image data:
    // - file.uint8Array (Uint8Array binary data)
    // - file.base64 (base64 string)
    // - file.mediaType (e.g. "image/png")
  }
}
```

Yes. The `rerank` function supports both strings and JSON objects as documents [3]. To get the best-matching object, use either:

1. The `rerankedDocuments` convenience array (first element is most relevant)
2. The `ranking` array (first element has the highest score)

Example with JSON objects:
```ts
import { rerank } from 'ai';
import { cohere } from '@ai-sdk/cohere';

const documents = [
  { id: 1, from: 'Paul Doe', subject: 'Follow-up', text: 'We are happy to give you a discount of 20% on your next order.' },
  { id: 2, from: 'John McGill', subject: 'Missing Info', text: 'Sorry, but here is the pricing information from Oracle: $5000/month' },
];

const { rerankedDocuments, ranking } = await rerank({
  model: cohere.reranking('rerank-v4.0-pro'),
  documents,
  query: 'Which pricing did we get from Oracle?',
  topN: 1,
});

// Best-matching object:
console.log(rerankedDocuments[0]); // { id: 2, from: 'John McGill', ... }
console.log(ranking[0].document);  // Same object
```

